From: "Harry S. Hawk" <habs@panix.com>
Date: Wed, 11 May 1994 16:54:57 -0400 (EDT)
Subject: [#94-5-160] Tech/Bus: MailWeir 2.0 Features

Here is the feature list for MailWeir 2.0; some folks have expressed
interest. If you received a copy of this privately this is a more
up to date version. It is still a draft so if you spot a typo please
let me know.

In a few days I will have a similar but expanded list of the features
for the next version (the C++ port). Let me know if you would like a
copy.

FYI, users on the main Extropians list are using the Best Value agent.

/hawk

		Version 2.0 (PERL) of MailWeir  - Feature List

  The PERL version of MailWeir contains 7 separate modules. 
  Simply, all incoming mail is recorded by the main module. The 
  main module databases each message and then passes the 
  message to one or more agents. Each agent then examines the 
  message and processes it for each of the users it supports. 
  Administrative functions are located both in their own modules 
  and distributed as needed in other modules including both the  
  main and agent modules.

Main Module:

   *	Databases each incoming message

   *	Assigns a unique id to each message (e.g., YYYY-MM-Msg#) - the
	command syntax allows for messages from the current month to
	be referred to just by the Msg#, the current years messages by
	MM-Msg#, and any archived message by the full Id, YYYY-MM-Msg#

   *	Stores information about each message (length, poster, etc.)

   *	contains dbm records of each list member

   *	processes administrative commands

   *	supports an administrator configured message size/line length
	limit - messages over the limit are cached in the database and
	the first x lines (default is 20 lines) of the message is
	sent. Users can request the message if they are interested 
	(::resend).

   *	supports an administrator configured message size/line length
	minimum. For example, messages of less than 3 lines are
	ignored and deleted. Used to filter out null messages.

   *	nosend messages (::nosend). Allows a message to be cached into
	the database and the poster is sent a acknowledgment
	containing the first few lines of the message and its Msg#. It
	appears on each users list of unsent messages. The nosend
	poster can post an additional message(s) indicating the
	Msg # and suggest people get it if they are interested.
	Allows users to post large messages with topical but perhaps
	limited appeal without wasting bandwidth.

   *	by default RFC 822 messages are addressed with a
	reply-to-field that directs replies to the e-mailing list
	rather then the poster of the message.

   *	tracks sent and unsent messages

   *	list (::list) of messages. Users can send a command which will
	return a list of each message in the cache; it lists each
	subject, message number, subject line and its poster.

   *	sends a filter-list; a list of unsent messages. users are sent
	a daily message containing all of the messages they have NOT
	received.  It contains the subject line, the poster and the
	Msg#.  optionally it can include the first five lines of
	each message.

   *	supports administrative messages that will be sent to all
	users on the list regardless of their agent settings. This
	requires the message be sent from a particular address and
	contain a certain prefix in the subject line (Admin: foo.bar).

   *	option to limit posting to those e-mail address that are in
	the master dbm; the allows for a closed list. Optionally all
	messages from non-list members can be bounced back to the
	originator with an explanation. A second option allows the
	message to be posted by sends a special acknowledgment to the
	list administrator.

   *	a translation table which allows users to post from address
	that are not contained in the master dbm.

   *	a blacklist table which present any e-mail address in it from
	posting regardless if that address is in the master dbm or
	not.  Useful for ejecting rowdy list members and from
	preventing e- mail loops from bouncing messages. (e.g., from a
	sendmail that doesn't send errors to the address listed in
	errors-to field).

   *	the size in bytes of the messages that can be kept can be set
	by the administrator. The administrator can also sent the
	number of days that messages are to be retained. The byte
	limit takes precedence over the retain date insuring that the
	hard drive is not filled up.  When this cache of messages is
	flushed it is done intelligently so that all pending digests
	are fulfilled (and sent) before the messages are deleted. This
	feature is most useful on system retention times due to
	traffic, limited disk space and/or a short retention time.

   *	Incoming subject lines are reformatted to remove "Re: " so
	that threads when sorted by topic will appear together.

   *	send private messages (::private); some users are not able to
	locate the e-mail address of message poster. The software has
	a syntax for allowing a message to be sent privately to any
	list member and defaults to the originator of the message
	being replied to.  This messages are acknowledged (if ::notify
	on).  The acknowledgment includes the first 5 lines of the
	message.
	
   *	the notify and verbose and quiet commands allow a user
	to set command acknowledgment to on or off. Users can also
	turn off the list of unsent messages. There is no
	acknowledgment for posters; posters see their own posts unless
	the have excluded themselves.

Administration:

  The administration of the mailing list can take place when either the
  administrator is logged into the host computer (with the appropriate
  login/password) or remotely via any RFC 822 e- mail message if sent
  from a particular pre-set address and optionally containing a password
  and/or a digital signature.

   *	a simple script is used to create a new list. It prompts the
	administrator for all the requirement information and has an
	option to supply information that is of a more advance and
	thus more technical level. Alias files and other system set-up
	still must be done but the script will indicate what to do.

   *	the configuration file that results from the install script
	can be edited directly by anyone who has the appropriate
	permissions.

   *	supports multiple administers (e.g., valid address from which
	administrative commands can be sent from).

   *	supports configurable RFC 822 compliant errors-to field so
	that bounced messages and error messages can be sent to any
	valid Internet address.

   *	all security breaches and other warns messages are sent to all
	administrators.
	
   *	with PowerServ users can automatically join and/or leave a
	list.  If it is a closed list the administrator is notified.

   *	without PowerServ administrators can maintain complete control
	over who joins and leaves the list.
	
   *	users can be added and deleted and the agent profiles altered
	from either the command line or via a simple command syntax
	that can be sent via any RFC 822 e-mail message.
	
   *	a PERL script that can be built into ELM can allow a
	administrator to add and delete users with only 5 key strokes,
	while allowing the administrator to manually over ride each
	add/drop.

   *	optional accounting features. The administrator sets up
	accounting classes (e.g., the cost of each type of
	subscription such as student, VIP, etc.). The administrator
	also sets the period of the subscription, text for dunning
	letters, free trial period if any, and other accounting
	related information.  When joins the list they are sent a
	e-bills.  Automatically they will get dunning letters and
	ultimately if they don't pay they are automatically deleted
	from the list. When their subscription period is up (e.g., a
	year later) they are again sent a e-bill and dunning letters.
	Except for entering payments the entire accounting system is
	fully automated.  There is unimplemented support to debit
	users for the actually CPU, and bandwidth they use and/or to
	bill full for certain command on a flat rate basis.


Simple Agent - uses least amount of CPU:

   *	resend messages by message number, subject line keyword, or
	subject line regular expression (::resend)

   *	contains dbm records of all users who it is assigned to.

   *	contains dbm profile for each users

   *	supports real-time "reflection mode"

   *	supports a single size digest function which defaults to 50k


Best-Value Agent - moderate CPU usage:

   *	all the features of the Simple Agent

   *	supports a variable size digest function 10 to 99 bytes

   *	support exclude/include:

   *	by subject line (keyword or regular expression)

   *	by poster (e-mail address or regular expression)

   *	Also can be set to include or exclude all messages

   *	takes advantage RFC 822 compliant mail readers. It uses the
	message ID, in-reply-to, and reply-to fields to simplify the
	command syntax. For example if a user send an exclude command
	(::exclude) the agent will automatically be able to determine
	what thread to exclude.  If the user sends "::exclude poster"
	the agent will automatically determine which poster to
	exclude.


VIP Agent - the full featured agent:

  The VIP agent has all of the features of the Basic Value agent plus:

   *	a keyword search command. This uses the agrep function. A user
	supplies a keyword and all messages in the current cache are
	examined.  Agrep is fuzzy in that it allows for 2 mismatches
	allowing for spelling mistakes, etc. Every message that
	matches is sent to the users.
	
   *	a WAIS search command. This is similar to the keyword search
	command except it uses the WAIS search functions. It returns
	every matching message to the requester.


Suitability:

   *	Usable by large companies

   *	Also works for small groups, companies and individuals

   *	Enhances spontaneous communication


