From: Erich Stefan Boleyn <erich@gemini.mth.pdx.edu>
Date: Wed, 11 May 94 13:55:04 -0700
Subject: [#94-5-162] Is AI possible? 

nancy@genie.slhs.udel.edu writes:

>  Derek Zahn <derek@cs.wisc.edu> writes: 
> 
> >Mr. John M. Casey:
> >
> >> There's a book by Roger Penrose called The Emperor's New Mind, that
> >> discusses in painstaking detail the possibility of computers (incl robots)
> >> ever being able to truly make the leaps of logic that can enable a human
> >> to, for instance, prove or disprove a theorem.
> >
> >Not at all.  I never cease to be amazed that anybody takes Penrose's
> >book to be anything at all relevant to any discussion about the
> >abilities of computers.  My somewhat careful reading of his
> >text doesn't reveal anything more than a transparent restatement
> >of Lucas's misguided application of Godel's Theorem to the "problem"
> >of consciousness.

Penrose gave some arguments that I think it is possible to show are
fundamentally flawed.  The only real "proof" he does is comparing a
human to a Turing machine, and arguing that a human is not a turing
machine by using a diagonalization method.  I pretty clearly
remember finding some crucial flaws in that "proof".  Details can
be provided to those interested.  The rest of the book wasn't
based on any kind of rigorous method at all.  It seemed to
be speculation (based on his earlier "proof" that humans are
not turing machines), that you could still get computational AI
allowing for quantum effects of some sort.

On the whole, I agree that it seems strange that people take Penrose's
word so completely, when his arguments claim to provide proof, but
generally are not rigorous.  I tend to think some of it is self-
fulfilling prophecy, IMHO, because people want to hear that there
is some kind of "free-will" that is not deterministic.  Many people
I've talked to just say "it can't be", and then shudder.  Clearly
they find the possibility threatening, though I'm sure that is not
always the case.

