-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

There seems to be quite an interest in filtering lately. New software
that we could all use appears on the way from Hawk and Ray. Voting on
postings so the results could be applied to information filtering 
algorithms of others who have not yet read the post might be useful,
but I must say I ignored most of the discussion on this thread until
the last few posts, because I didn't feel there would be much use
of it except in how it might evolve to apply to our own list, wrt
how to do ::excludes etc. It would be of little use because by the 
time it had any effect, the threads would have died or mutated.
It might be of greater use to exclude specific people. 

Although this may change in the future, I have never bothered to
use any ::excludes. It might be okay for threads, and if it were
available on Cypherpunks, I could use it there immediately. I'm
not averse to using kill files on usenet newsgroups, but I don't
use the news reader to read my mail. This, too, might change...

But is this not an aspect of Reputations?  Are we discussing, or
could we discuss, the development of filtering agents that react
to the reputations of posters?

I know many Extropians are also Cypherpunks, but "over there" the
other day Tim May was asking about HEx, so I explained to him that
Brian Hawthorne had found the Extropian volume too high and 
announced that he was leaving the List, and that he had sold 
the Hawthorne Exchange to me for a small fee. Although I haven't
checked yet, I'm sure Brian should have received his payment in
the snail mail by now, and as soon as I can find a place from 
which to run this unix-based software, I will download it all
and figure out what to do with it. I mentioned to Cypherpunks
that Extropians had not exactly been crying out for the ressurection
of HEx. In fact, it seems to have gone into limbo without many
people noticing.

Tim mentioned that there were several problems with HEx, and he
has posted here about that in the past. He seems to have remembered
manipulating the values of reputations to amass a large volume
of thornes. Tim seems to be very good at amassing large volumes
of various currencies. He was also _buying_ thornes, to try to
get some real value established, I believe, and only one Extropian
took him up on the idea, for $10. 

Well, yes, there were problems with HEx. It was a first experiment,
I believe, although there may be other reputation markets around.
In the "real" world, the reputation markets are the credit reporting
agencies and the direct marketing mailing list industry. Perhaps
there are other good examples. I do not know if there are any other
reputation markets on the Internet, although there are definitely
Internet reputations, witness McElwaine, Detweiller, Sternlight,
and the latest perversion, based on the movie "Lassie Go Home".

There was the problem that HEx itself would _give_ so many thornes
to new Customers. That the value of thornes had no basis in reality
was another problem. That Customers could cause wild swings in
values of reputations by currency manipulation was another. That
there were no transaction fees applied, meaning that anyone could
execute "voting" by buying and selling shares without paying for
the privilege was another problem. I didn't think the system 
worked because it didn't seem properly oriented - who is going
to buy reputations of someone whose posts they dislike, so that
they might sell them to devalue them? Backwards, IMO. Maybe I
misunderstood how this was supposed to work. I know I was tempted
to ::exclude the HEx Nightly Market Report, although I would read
it once in a while.

Anyway, I would like to receive any feedback or suggestions from
Extropians about HEx. Should I revive it as a market for the 
reputations of Extropians?  There are some things I want to do,
such as offer Reputation Services through my soon-to-be operating
INFO_Banque, where "soon" is not particularly well defined. There
will be a market for reputations, so I would like to do some work
on this concept to see if I could make a little digital cash for
myself. As far as I'm concerned at this point in time (oh, a
phrase I _hate_ arrggggh) the Thorne will remain the Thorne,
and any reincarnation of HEx in any format similar to the last
ie first version will remain something exclusively for Extropians,
but the INFO_Banque will in the future try to do some reputation
marketing - hopefully in some better way - to a wider market.

If there is no interest in reviving the Thorne and HEx, I will
get on with other things, but I should have an access point to
the internet over which I will have control in the current fiscal
year. At minimum, I will be able to put up an old pc running linux
or minix or something like that to operate the system. I also have
to learn how to use unix. I may have a better machine available,
too, but we'll have to see how the Day Job goes. After all, I _do_
need to devise a method whereby, say, medical students and faculty 
and staff (all my Day Job Customers) can filter their e-mail, to 
cut down on their research and study time, to improve their reading 
by allowing them to filter mail based on reputations, heh heh, you 
see... I need to experiment...

Meanwhile, some ideas to toss around :

Reputations, when newly created, are clean.

If someone wants to sully a reputation, by issuing a "Condemn"
transaction at cost of digital postage, they may do so. They 
must be members of the Exchange to do this, and they must provide 
some credible reason for doing so. Their own reputations will be 
affected by their actions - of course - but here we must be careful 
to observe that two meanings of the word "repuation" have occurred. 
That is, there is the reputation of someone by virtue of what some 
people might think of him or her, and there is the market item in the
reputation market. This market item must somehow include the fact
that the reputation owner of note has made condemnations. Everything
in the market would be open to everyone participating. I guess since
transaction issuers must be members themselves means their reputations
can be directly affected by anyone with a digital postage stamp.

Similarly, members could enhance the reputations of others by
making "Approve" or "Praise be" transactions. I'll have to do
some creative thinking about other types of reputation transactions
that could be useful. Different types of transactions could have
different processing fees, like one unit for an Approve, but two
to five units for "Praise Be"'s, depending upon the number of stars
the praisor wants to apply. "Condemns" would cost more than
"Oh, my"'s but less than "This is a net.loonie"'s.

Receipt of a certain number of net.loonie transactions could force
a reputation's STATUS to be "Net.loonie". Receipt of enough 5-star
appraisals could force STATUS to be "*****".

Some parameters needed for reputations :
REP_OWNER - who Registered the Reputation in the Market, and paid
   the registration fee.
REP_NAME  - who or what is the entity whose reputation is being
   registered known as,
REP_ADDR  - e-mail address of the reputation - this may be something
   that would have to be looked at from a privacy point of view.
REP_INIT  - the initial abstract from the reputation owner as to
   why he or she registered the reputation in the first place.
   This field could perhaps be modified by either the OWNER or
   the Exchange, in case the owner wants to edit the original
   because it was created in error or because things have 
   changed, or by the Exchange itself if it determines or it
   becomes known through other transactions that the Initial
   Abstract contains false, erroneous, slanderous, or libelous
   information that should not be maintained in the Market.
REP_TX_LIST - a reference or pointer to Reputation Transactions
   executed by market members on this reputation.
REP_STATUS - a capsule summary of the Reputation in the paridigm
   of the users. Some algoritmic processor would scan the TX_LIST
   upon execution of any Tx to compute the latest STATUS, but this
   would not necessarily mean that any single new Condemn Tx could,
   for example, change a 5-star reputation.

   There may be need of more than one summary of reputation parameter,
   since financial reputations would be different from posting
   reputations, but that may just mean there would have to be

REP_TYPES...

